        "The intended value has two parts:\n1. Presentation: clearer, more attractive imagery may help a listing stand out and encourage enquiries.\n2. Time: a walkthrough can help prospective buyers or guests understand the layout before arranging a viewing. People who realise the property is not suitable may decide not to enquire, potentially saving the agent time.\n2.1 Find suitable listings\nLook through property listing platforms such as Airbnb, Zillow, Property24, or Booking.com, depending on the market you serve.",
        "Look for listings where the images appear to have obvious presentation problems:\n- Dark or uneven lighting.\n- Poor framing.\n- Unflattering angles.\n- Cluttered or visually confusing composition.\n- Inconsistent image quality across the listing.\nDo not assume every imperfect photograph is a suitable prospect. Some properties may genuinely be difficult to photograph, and an AI edit cannot fix every underlying issue.\nImportant permission rule\nThe source demonstrates taking photographs from public listings and using them to create samples. Public visibility does not automatically mean you have permission to download, modify, or commercially reuse those images.",
        "A safer workflow is to contact the agent or owner first, request permission to create a sample, or use images supplied by the prospective client. Do not publish or distribute modified property images without the appropriate rights and approval.\n2.2 Improve the photographs\nUse an AI image-generation or image-editing platform capable of working from supplied photographs. The source names Higgsfield as an example.",
        "A practical workflow:\n1. Obtain the original images with permission.\n2. Select a small number of representative photographs.\n3. Upload them to the image tool.\n4. Describe the improvements required.\n5. Generate revised versions.\n6. Compare each result with its source.\n7. Reject outputs that misrepresent the property.\n8. Share the approved before-and-after samples with the client.\nThe goal is to improve presentation, not to create a different property.\nWhat to check\n- Are the room dimensions and proportions preserved?\n- Are windows, doors, fixtures, and architectural features still accurate?\n- Has the tool added furniture, decor, views, or amenities that do not exist?\n- Has it removed a permanent defect or altered the condition of the property?\n- Does the image still represent the actual room?\n- Are colours and lighting plausible rather than misleading?\nIf an AI edit changes material facts, do not present it as a faithful property photograph. Clearly label any conceptual staging or illustrative imagery, and follow the relevant listing-platform rules.\n2.3 Add a cinematic walkthrough\nThe source identifies a walkthrough video generated from the same property photographs as the premium extension.",
