        "Accuracy: Preserve the original meaning. Flag unclear or unsupported claims rather than filling gaps.",
        "Editorial quality: Avoid generic hooks, repetitive phrasing, artificial enthusiasm, and obvious AI language.",
        "Platform fit: Adapt structure and length to each format rather than copying the same paragraph everywhere.\n3.5 Turn one source into several assets\nThe source demonstrates generating four broad content types from a single video:\nOutput\tPurpose\tWhat to preserve\nNewsletter\tDevelop the main idea for an owned audience\tArgument, context, examples, useful takeaways\nShort-form scripts\tPresent one idea in a concise spoken format\tOne clear point, a strong opening, coherent explanation\nX posts\tShare concise observations and ideas\tSpecific insight and natural voice\nLinkedIn posts\tExplain professional or business implications\tContext, reasoning, practical relevance",
        "The model can create a first draft quickly. The final quality still depends on human review.\nA practical repurposing sequence\n1. Ask the AI to identify the source's main thesis.\n2. Extract the distinct supporting ideas.\n3. Group related points into themes.\n4. Choose which themes suit each platform.\n5. Generate drafts in the client's voice.\n6. Check every claim against the transcript or approved source.\n7. Edit for clarity, originality, and platform fit.\n8. Deliver the content in an organised document or publishing calendar.\nDo not force every transcript to produce the same number of assets. A short or narrow source may not contain enough distinct material for a large content package.\n3.6 Quality control is the paid work\nBefore sending the sample or publishing any content:\n- Read every item.\n- Verify names, numbers, and factual claims.\n- Check that quotations are exact if quotation marks are used.\n- Make sure the original meaning has not been changed.\n- Remove repetitive ideas across the deliverables.\n- Confirm that each piece sounds like the client.\n- Check that the piece makes sense without the full source.\n- Confirm that the client has rights to the source material.\nThe service is not “paste a transcript into AI.” It is “turn existing expertise into accurate, voice-consistent, platform-ready content.”\n3.7 Win the first client with a relevant sample\n1. Find a creator who publishes regularly.\n2. Identify a platform or format they underuse.\n3. Choose one recent public episode or a source the creator has authorised you to use.\n4. Create a small sample, such as one newsletter draft and a few posts.\n5. Edit it carefully.\n6. Send the sample with a short explanation of the distribution opportunity.\n7. Offer a paid monthly package if the creator finds it useful.\nAvoid sending a huge unsolicited content bundle. A focused, high-quality sample is easier to evaluate.\n3.8 Pricing and recurring work\nThe source suggests that clients may pay from a few hundred dollars to a couple of thousand dollars per month, depending on volume and quality. Treat this as an illustrative range, not a market guarantee.",
