        "This model is different from buying a cold list. The source explicitly says to work only with the business's own leads who previously opted in to hear from it.\n4.1 Understand the business and its lead history\nPotential customers include businesses that receive enquiries and book appointments, such as:\n- Dental clinics.\n- Gyms.\n- Roofers.\n- Medical spas.\n- Other local service businesses.\nBefore accepting a project, understand:\n- Where the leads came from.\n- When they were collected.\n- What consent was obtained.\n- Which channels the person agreed to use.\n- Whether the lead has already opted out.\n- What products or services the business currently offers.\n- What counts as a successful conversion.\nDo not assume that a contact in a spreadsheet is automatically eligible for marketing messages.\n4.2 Prepare the lead list\nThe source demonstrates importing a list into GoHighLevel (HighLevel).",
        "A typical workflow is:\n1. Obtain the lead data from the business's CRM or spreadsheet.\n2. Confirm the business has the right to contact each person.\n3. Remove duplicates and incomplete records.\n4. Exclude people who opted out or should not be contacted.\n5. Organise the records into a usable format.\n6. Import the eligible contacts into the messaging platform.\n7. Keep a record of the source and status of each contact.\nUse only the minimum personal data needed to perform the service. Handle it securely and agree with the client how it will be accessed, stored, and deleted.\n4.3 Draft a natural reactivation message\nThe source gives an example for a dental clinic:\n“Hey Sarah, it's Bright Smiles Dental. We came across your details from a while back. Are you still interested to get that checkup sorted?”",
