      "id": "chapter-10",
      "number": 10,
      "title": "Turn a One-Off Job into a Reliable Service",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The source repeatedly points toward monthly retainers as a way to move beyond isolated projects.",
        "A retainer works only when the customer has an ongoing need and the service provider can define what will be delivered.\n10.1 Identify the recurring activity\nOne-off project\tPossible recurring service\nEnhance one property listing\tProcess a defined number of listings each month\nRepurpose one podcast episode\tProduce content packages for each new episode\nReactivate one old-lead segment\tRun agreed campaigns and report eligible outcomes\nRedesign one website\tProvide defined maintenance and updates\nCreate one ad\tProduce a monthly batch of creative variations\nInstall one support assistant\tMaintain the knowledge base and test changes",
        "Not every service naturally needs a retainer. Do not invent monthly work that the client does not need.\n10.2 Define the scope\nA good service agreement specifies:\n- Deliverables.\n- Monthly quantity or usage limits.\n- Turnaround times.\n- Client responsibilities.\n- Revision limits.\n- Approval process.\n- Tool and third-party costs.\n- Ownership and usage rights.\n- Data access and security.\n- Support availability.\n- Cancellation terms.\n- What counts as additional work.\n10.3 Track the economics\nFor each client, track:\n- Revenue received.\n- Tool subscriptions allocated to the client.\n- Generation credits.\n- Other direct expenses.\n- Hours spent on delivery.\n- Hours spent on revisions and support.\n- Acquisition and onboarding time.\n- Payment fees and taxes where relevant.\nA service can look profitable when only AI credits are counted while consuming many hours of unpaid labour.",
        "A simple calculation:",
        "Contribution before overhead and tax = client revenue − direct delivery costs",
        "Also calculate:",
        "Effective hourly return = contribution before overhead and tax ÷ total hours spent",
        "Include sales, onboarding, communication, revisions, and maintenance when estimating total time.\n10.4 Build repeatable processes\nDocument the steps for each service:\n1. Client onboarding.\n2. Required information and permissions.\n3. Tool configuration.\n4. Production workflow.\n5. Quality-control checklist.\n6. Client approval.\n7. Delivery.\n8. Reporting.\n9. Maintenance.\n10. Offboarding and data deletion.\nRepeatability makes the service easier to deliver consistently and easier to improve."
      ]
